r the public to  know that you guys are doing on your end  to ensure our safety but that we should  be aware of absolutely we we're really  diligent about  reminding our community about the  dangers of flash flooding but most  people maybe those who've lived here for  a while have forgotten about the risk  one and then we have a lot of new  residents who are living here now and  may not understand that we do get flash  flooding and hopefully with these last  few days they're starting to see a  little bit more rain a little bit more  water flowing through the storm channels  and they're becoming aware of it and the  last group of people who are  on our radar as well are new drivers who  may not realize that there is a risk of  driving into flooded waters yeah if  you're not aware of the conditions  especially in our city i think it's a  little unique if people are coming from  elsewhere and just really quickly i want  to talk about every facility is designed  to handle a hundred year storm what does  that mean  so any day we could if it starts raining  we have a risk of having a hundred year  flood  you hear that term thrown around quite a  bit and basically it means that there's  a one percent chance any day of having a  large flood so if we had  two years ago we had a hundred year  flood that doesn't mean we won't have  one for 98 years it could happen today  it could happen tomorrow so we really  want to encourage the community to be  vigilant about flood waters they can go  to our website regionalflood.org to see  rainfall in their community we have  over 222 rain gauges around clark county  so we can really accurately predict how  much rain is happening in the community  itself well michelle thank you so much  for breaking this all down this is  really important information important  to keep in mind especially during  monsoon season so thank you for being  here and all the information is on your  screen thank you to the regional flood  control district for spon